NAME
====

**wattle** - the Wattle programming language

DESCRIPTION
===========

Wattle is a dynamic language with Clojure-like syntax. A program is parsed and
compiled into bytecode that is run on the Wattle virtual machine.

Wattle's virtual machine and core library derive from Janet. The parser accepts
Wattle source, not Janet source. Source files have the extension ``.wattle``.
This page describes the language; `wattle(1)` describes the command-line
program.

A small program illustrates definitions, functions, collections and calls:

````
(defn greeting [name]
  (string "Hello, " name "!"))

(defn main [script & args]
  (each name (if (empty? args) ["world"] args)
    (print (greeting name))))
````

Running this file with `wattle(1)` from the command line prints ``Hello,
<argument>!`` for each command line argument, or ``Hello, world!`` if there are
no arguments.

SOURCE FORMS
============

Wattle source consists of forms, whitespace and comments. The forms below
include tokens, byte sequences, collections and tuples. Prefixes modify the
form that follows them.

Comments
--------

A semicolon begins a comment that ends at the end of the line. A ``#!`` at the
first byte of a file also begins a shebang comment, for a script's interpreter
line.

Whitespace
----------

Whitespace includes spaces, tabs, line breaks and commas. Commas may separate
forms, as in ``{:a 1, :b 2}``; they are never part of a symbol.

Tokens
------

Tokens comprise literal tokens, numbers, symbols and keywords.

There are three literal tokens: ``true``, ``false`` and ``nil``. In a
conditional test, only ``false`` and ``nil`` are false. Zero, an empty string
and empty collections are all treated as true.

Numbers are double-precision floating-point values. Decimal, exponent,
hexadecimal and radix forms include ``42``, ``-1.5``, ``1e3``, ``0x1F`` and
``2r101``. Underscores may separate digits, as in ``1_000``. There is no
separate integer literal type. ``int/s64`` and ``int/u64`` construct exact
64-bit integer values when needed.

A symbol is a name formed from ASCII letters, digits, valid non-ASCII UTF-8 and
the characters ``!$%&*+-./:<=>?@^_``. Examples include ``total``,
``string/split`` and ``empty?``. A symbol cannot begin with a digit or colon.
At the start of a form, ``@`` and ``^`` are parse errors; reserve ``!`` for
mutable literals. A token that reads as a number is a number. Symbols are
resolved as bindings unless quoted.

A keyword begins with a colon, as in ``:name``. Keywords are values, commonly
used as map keys.

Byte sequences
--------------

A string is a sequence of bytes delimited by double quotes. Strings are
immutable. Strings can include escapes like ``\n``, ``\t``, ``\"`` and ``\\``;
``\xHH`` inserts a byte, and ``\uXXXX`` inserts a Unicode code point encoded as
UTF-8.

A buffer uses ``!`` immediately before its opening double quotes. Buffers are
mutable byte sequences and accept the same escapes as strings.

Three or more double quotes delimit a *raw* string or buffer. Raw byte
sequences may span lines and do not process escapes. A line break immediately
after the opening quotes and one before the closing quotes are removed. If
every content line has the opening quotes' indentation, that indentation is
removed from each line.

````
(type "a\nb")                ; :string
(type """raw\n""")           ; :string
(type !"a\nb")               ; :buffer
(type !"""raw\n""")          ; :buffer

(length "e")                 ; 1 byte
(length "é")                 ; 2 bytes

(def text
  """
  first
  second
  """)
(= text "first\nsecond")     ; true
(= "\\n" """\n""")           ; true: raw \n is two bytes
````

Collections
-----------

Wattle includes five built-in collections:

````
[a b]         ; immutable vector
{:a :b}       ; immutable map
#{:a :b}      ; immutable set

![a b]        ; mutable array
!{:a :b}      ; mutable table
````

Vectors, arrays and sets store elements. Maps and tables store key-value pairs.
Each element, key and value is an expression evaluated when the collection is
constructed. Quoting a form gives its syntax as data.

Function calls
--------------

Parentheses delimit a tuple. A tuple is an immutable sequence of values. In
executable source, the compiler treats a tuple as a function call, a special
form or a macro invocation. In ``(+ 1 2)``, ``+`` is called with two arguments
and returns 3. The function position may itself contain a form: ``((fn [x] (* x
x)) 4)`` returns 16.

Tuples are primarily used to represent Wattle code rather than general-purpose
sequential data; use a vector for ordinary sequential data. Quoting a tuple
produces its value without compiling it as a call: ``'(+ 1 2)`` contains the
symbol ``+`` and the numbers 1 and 2.

Prefixes
--------

Four prefixes modify the form that follows:

````
'x     ; shorthand for (quote x)
`x     ; shorthand for (quasiquote x)
~x     ; shorthand for (unquote x)
|x     ; shorthand for (splice x)
````

The form after a prefix must start on the same line.

COLLECTIONS
===========

Wattle includes immutable and mutable collections.

Mutability
----------

Vectors, maps and sets are persistent, immutable collections. Operations that
add, remove or replace values return an updated collection and leave the
original available. The result may share unchanged structure with the
original, so an update need not copy the entire collection.

````
(def xs [1 2])
(def ys (conj xs 3))
xs                    ; [1 2]
ys                    ; [1 2 3]
(assoc {:a 1} :b 2)   ; {:a 1 :b 2}
````

Arrays and tables are mutable. Operations such as ``put!`` and ``array/push!``
change them in place:

````
(def items ![1 2])
(array/push! items 3)
items                 ; ![1 2 3]
````

Arrays and tables compare by identity. Vectors, maps and sets compare by their
contents.

Accessing
---------

Vectors and arrays are indexed from zero. ``get`` reads a position or key and
accepts an optional default:

````
(get [10 20] 1)        ; 20
(get [10 20] 2 :oops)  ; :oops
````

Map and table literals alternate key and value expressions. Neither type can
store ``nil`` or NaN as a key. Storing ``nil`` as a value removes the entry.
Set elements also cannot be ``nil`` or NaN, and duplicate elements count once.
Map and set iteration order is unspecified; do not rely on the order of
expression evaluation inside their literals. ``has-value?`` checks set
membership:

````
(get {:name "Ada"} :name)        ; "Ada"
(get {:name "Ada"} :age :oops)   ; :oops
(has-value? #{1 2} 2)            ; true
````

A collection can also be called with an index or key to look up a value:

````
([10 20] 1)             ; 20
({:name "Ada"} :name)   ; "Ada"
(#{1 2 3} 2)            ; 2
````

BINDINGS AND CONTROL FLOW
=========================

``def`` binds a value to a name that cannot be reassigned. ``var`` binds a
value to a name that ``set`` can reassign. Both accept destructuring patterns.
``do`` evaluates its forms in order and returns the last result. The core
library offers the ``let`` macro that uses these to create bindings within a
specified scope:

````
(def raise 7)
(var total raise)
(set total (+ total 3))
(let [x 4, y 5]
  (+ x y total))          ; 19
````

Patterns
--------

``def``, ``var``, ``let`` and function parameters can destructure values.
Vector and tuple patterns bind by position. A map pattern writes the binding
first and the key second:

````
(def [first second & rest] [1 2 3 4])
(def {name :name} {:name "Ada"})
(def {:keys [x y]} {:x 1 :y 2})
````

In a map pattern, ``:keys [x y]`` selects keyword keys. ``:strs`` and ``:syms``
select string and symbol keys; ``:as`` binds the whole value; and ``:or {x
default}`` supplies a default when ``x`` is ``nil``. Defaults are evaluated
only when used.

Conditionals
------------

``if`` evaluates its test and one branch: ``(if test then else)``. The core
library defines ``when`` for a conditional body, ``cond`` for successive tests
and ``case`` for comparing one value with several candidates:

````
(if (> total 0) :positive :nonpositive)  ; :positive
(when (> total 0) :positive)             ; :positive
(cond (< total 0) :negative
      (= total 0) :zero
      :positive)                         ; :positive
(case total 0 :zero 10 :ten :other)      ; :ten
````

Loops
-----

``while`` tests its condition before each repetition and evaluates its body
while the condition is truthy. ``break`` exits the loop. The core library
defines ``loop``, ``for`` and ``each`` for iteration:

````
(each item [1 2 3]
  (print item))
````

FUNCTIONS
=========

``fn`` creates a function. The core library defines ``defn`` to bind a named
function with optional metadata and a docstring. Parameter lists are vectors. A
symbol after ``&`` receives the remaining arguments as a vector. Functions are
values: they may be passed, stored and returned, and they can refer to bindings
in their enclosing scope.

````
(def double (fn [x] (* x 2)))
(double 3)                ; 6

(defn sum [xs]
  (reduce + 0 xs))

(defn add-all [& numbers]
  (sum numbers))

(add-all 1 2 3)           ; 6
````

A function can have several arities. Each clause begins with a parameter
vector. A call selects the clause for its argument count. Clauses cannot
duplicate or skip counts, and at most one may use ``&``:

````
(defn greet
  "Returns a greeting."
  ([] (greet "world"))
  ([name] (string "Hello, " name "!")))
````

For named options, a map pattern after ``&`` receives keyword and value pairs.
A final map may supply or override those pairs:

````
(defn greet-with [name & {:keys [prefix] :or {prefix "Hello"}}]
  (string prefix ", " name "!"))

(greet-with "Ada" :prefix "Hi")    ; "Hi, Ada!"
(greet-with "Ada" {:prefix "Hi"})  ; "Hi, Ada!"
````

A short function has the form ``#(...)``. Within it, ``$`` or ``$0`` is the
first argument, ``$1`` the second, and ``$&`` the remaining arguments after the
highest numbered argument used:

````
(map #(* $ 2) [1 2 3])  ; ![2 4 6]
````

MACROS
======

A macro receives its arguments as unevaluated forms and returns a form for the
compiler. The core library provides ``defmacro`` for defining macros.
Quasiquote (`` ` ``) constructs a form. Within it, ``~x`` inserts one form,
while ``~|args`` inserts each form from a sequence:

````
(defmacro twice [x]
  `(do ~x ~x))

(defmacro call-with [f & args]
  `(~f ~|args))

(var calls 0)
(twice (set calls (+ calls 1)))
calls                            ; 2
(call-with + 1 2)                ; 3
````

``twice`` inserts its argument twice, so the argument is evaluated twice when
the expanded form runs.

Parentheses in generated code are tuples. Vectors, maps and sets remain
collections in the syntax tree, so a macro can inspect the form written in
source. ``tuple`` can be used to construct a call without quasiquote.

MODULES
=======

A module can be a Wattle source file, an image or a native library. ``import``
loads a module and adds its public bindings to the current environment with a
prefix derived from the module name. ``use`` adds the bindings without a
prefix.

If ``greetings.wattle`` defines ``hello``, either of these forms makes it
available:

````
(import ./greetings)
(greetings/hello "Ada")

(use ./greetings)
(hello "Ada")
````

``import`` also accepts ``:as`` or ``:prefix`` to choose the prefix.
`WATTLE_PATH` selects directories for system modules; see `wattle(1)`.

ERRORS
======

``(error value)`` raises ``value`` as an error. ``try`` evaluates one body
expression and returns its result if it succeeds. If the body raises, the
handler's ``[e]`` binds the raised value, and ``try`` returns the handler's
result:

````
(try 42
  ([e] 0))                 ; 42

(try (error "failed")
  ([e] e))                 ; "failed"
````

SEE ALSO
========

`wattle(1)`

The REPL's ``(doc)`` lists available bindings, and ``(doc symbol)`` describes
one. Example programs are distributed with the Wattle source tree.

HISTORY
=======

Wattle began as a fork of Janet, the language created by Calvin Rose.
